lifeos-nix patch f-private-zones — private zones: counted, never quoted

PROBLEM
  Archive probes run over ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl, i.e. over EVERY project directory,
  and routinely print examples. Some directories hold material that must not enter an
  unrelated session's context: work under NDA, medical or legal notes, anything personal.
  Upstream has no way to declare such a zone, so the only thing standing between a sweep and
  that material is the operator remembering.

  The boundary is not reading, it is CONTENT EGRESS. An aggregate is safe (totals, per-day
  counts, durations); an excerpt is not. And the distinction that survives scrutiny is
  NARRATED vs RAW: a curated first-person hand-off note may cross a threshold, a raw
  transcript may not.

SOLUTION
  - LIFEOS/TOOLS/PrivateZones.ts — the register-driven classifier and the actual protection.
    Archive probes call splitBySensitivity/bucketLabel, which count every zone without ever
    naming one. Absent register = feature OFF (opt-in). Present but malformed = FAIL CLOSED.
    Private beats public whatever the pattern lengths; narrated artifacts cross even from
    inside a zone; unregistered project dirs are quote-closed and count-open. `--unregistered`
    lists what awaits triage, warns (exit 3) when a register is declared with no hook wired,
    and exits non-zero on an unusable register.
  - hooks/PrivateZoneEgressGuard.hook.ts — the backstop on the tools whose path is a FIELD:
    Read, Grep, Glob, NotebookRead. Classify the field, refuse anything not quotable. No
    tokenising, no verb lists, no glob semantics.
  - USER/CONFIG/private-zones.example.json + hooks.user.json — the register template and the
    registration, in the ours-zone overlay that MergeSettings composes over the official
    hooks.json.

SCOPE, stated rather than half-guarded
  Bash is NOT covered. `cat <zone>/x` is not refused. An earlier version guarded it and
  twenty-five of twenty-seven review rounds went into deciding, from the text of an arbitrary
  shell command, what it would read; every fix opened a new spelling (quotes, sh -c, a
  two-step cd, *, ?, [st], {t,x}, wrapper words, redirections, a mkdir decoy that forged the
  own-room grant), and it twice broke real work with false positives. Parsing shell is not a
  sequence of bugs to fix, so the lane is gone and the promise is not made.

MODIFIES NO UPSTREAM FILE
  The patch only CREATES four files. Registration lives in the USER overlay, which is where
  f-mergesettings-hooks-overlay says our additions belong so hooks.json stays pure-official.
  default.nix carries a postPatch chmod, because `diff` cannot express a mode and a hook
  without +x fails 126 — read as a non-blocking error, i.e. a silent no-op.

KNOWN LIMITS
  - The example register and the overlay reach a fresh install through ScaffoldUser, which
    copy-MISSES: an install that already has hooks.user.json never receives ours. That is why
    `--unregistered` carries the wiring self-check, which reads the COMPOSED settings.json
    rather than the delivery path it is complaining about.
  - A rule is a path-SEGMENT pattern, not a root anchor. It REFUSES on a substring match but
    only GRANTS the own-room allowance on a path boundary, so a fragment-shaped rule blocks
    everywhere and grants nowhere.
  - Matching is textual for rules and resolved for paths (both spellings, symlinks included).
    A path assembled at runtime by a probe that does not use the library is not seen.

TESTS
  tests/private-zones/run.sh materialises this patch into a temp tree and runs the suite
  against it, including an end-to-end assertion through the real hook process.

